The Assert statement "Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0);" checks if the new index of value 'x' in ArrayList after setting is always greater than or equal to zero which is not possible as ArrayList index range is from 0 to Count-1.--------------------To extract the information embedded in comments starting with "@@@" and create the corresponding C# `Debug.Assert` statements from that, I'd recommend using the regex (regular expression) framework in .NET. In this case, we'll need to search for all lines that starts with "// @@@ ", then extract the comment and remove the prefix.

Here is the code to do that:

```cs
using System;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;

class Program
{
    private static readonly Regex AssertRegex = new Regex(@"^// @@@ (.*)$", RegexOptions.Multiline);

    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        string code = System.IO.File.ReadAllText("Your_Code_File_Path_Here");
        MatchCollection matches = AssertRegex.Matches(code);

        foreach (Match match in matches)
        {
            string message = match.Groups[1].Value;
            Console.WriteLine($"Debug.Assert({message});");
        }
    }
}
```

You need to replace `"Your_Code_File_Path_Here"` with the path of the `.cs` file you want to parse.

This program will print to console the corresponding `Debug.Assert` statements. Please, make sure to run this in an environment where Visual Studio is not installed or to have access to the `System.IO.File` class. If you run this and nothing is printed to the console, it means that no comments starting with "@@@" were found in the file.

Please note that Regex parsing is just a start and might not handle all cases. If your file is huge and contains a lot of code, a more complex parser might be needed. There might also be special characters that need to be escaped in your message.

Finally, be advised that if your code is not properly escaped, this might lead to a lot of security vulnerabilities and issues due to how the `Debug.Assert` works in C#, and its execution is controlled by the DEBUG conditional compilation symbol. 

